[codex] """E* -- the frozen reference evaluator for the circle-packing plumbing task.
[codex] 
[codex] E* is `Candidate -> Outcome` for the study's A0.1 evaluator-integration
[codex] conformance check.  It is frozen *outside* the runtimes under test: no runtime's
[codex] own provenance schema, cache, or log directory is part of this file's contract.
[codex] 
[codex] Objective (upstream definition, unchanged):
[codex] 
[codex]     sum_radii = sum of the radii of the circles returned by pack_circles(n)
[codex] 
[codex] `sum_radii` is the primary metric named by the upstream task itself
[codex] (`circle-packing/loreley.program.md:8-10`; objective in
[codex] `circle-packing/README.md:19-27`) and computed by Loreley's evaluator plugin
[codex] (`circle_packing_env/evaluate.py:133-145`).  E* keeps the formula bit-for-bit:
[codex] the sum runs over every returned circle in the order the candidate returned
[codex] them.  E* does not collapse it to a scalar -- the Outcome carries the objective
[codex] plus validity and structural fields; turning that into a search signal is the
[codex] adapter's job (`F_r`).
[codex] 
[codex] Three hard constraints from the study protocol (§4.2.2) and how they are met:
[codex] 
[codex] 1. No dependence on cwd or PATH.  Candidate code runs in a child process whose
[codex]    environment is fully specified by E* (`PATH` empty, no `PYTHONPATH`, private
[codex]    HOME/TMPDIR/cwd under E*'s state root) on a private copy of the candidate
[codex]    bytes.  E* itself never reads the environment into the Outcome.
[codex] 2. No counting or logging into a directory that can be reverted.  Every byte E*
[codex]    writes goes below its own state root (`--state-dir`, default under the system
[codex]    temp directory) -- never into the caller's tree, never into a candidate
[codex]    worktree -- so `git checkout` / `git clean` in a candidate cannot revert E*'s
[codex]    records, and E* cannot pollute a candidate's tree.
[codex] 3. Multi-field output.  The Outcome reports `status`, the objective, structural
[codex]    fields (overlap count, min gap, boundary margin, packing density) and the
[codex]    audit accounting instead of a single score.
[codex] 
[codex] Randomness: there is none beyond the explicit `--seed`, which draws the audit
[codex] subsample (decimation) for `--fidelity fast`.  `--fidelity full` audits every
[codex] item, so the seed is inert there by construction; for candidates whose only
[codex] defect sits in a decimated-away pair the seed flips the verdict, which is the
[codex] point: the seed is an evaluator input, not a label.
[codex] 
[codex] CLI:
[codex] 
[codex]     e_star.py evaluate --candidate <path> --seed <S> [--fidelity full|fast]
[codex]                        [--fraction F] --out <json> [--state-dir DIR]
[codex] 
[codex] Exit codes: 0 = ok, 2 = invalid (measured and rejected), 3 = error (no
[codex] measurement could be taken).  E* never emits 0.0 or any other number in place of
[codex] a failure, so an adapter cannot silently turn "could not evaluate" into a score.
[codex] """
[codex] 
